# Usability & Accessibility Review Agent

A Claude Code skill that logs into a live web application, captures a real page view, and produces a structured usability/accessibility report — combining an LLM's judgment on subjective design qualities with deterministic, code-computed measurements wherever a real measurement is possible. Built as a prototype to answer a specific question: *can an agent do a first-pass usability sweep that's fast and consistent, without quietly turning "accessibility review" into "the AI looked at a screenshot and said it seemed fine"?*

## Why this exists

Usability review is normally a manual, ad hoc pass — a designer or QA engineer eyeballing a page and writing up impressions. That doesn't scale, and it happens rarely (usually only around a launch), so regressions between reviews go unnoticed. The goal was to see whether a page review could be triggered on demand, run consistently, and — critically — be honest about which of its findings are genuine judgment calls versus things that were actually measured.

## Architecture

**Capture test (Playwright).** Reuses existing test infrastructure end to end: authenticates through the same login helper the functional test suite already uses, navigates to the target view, and takes a screenshot. Deliberately a *viewport-only* screenshot, not a full-page one — an early attempt at full-page capture produced an image over 13,000px tall because one page's main data table renders unpaginated, which is unreviewable at any sane resolution. Scroll depth is recorded as its own signal instead of forced into an unreviewable image.

**Signal-extraction script.** A small module run via Playwright's `page.evaluate`, executed against the live DOM immediately after capture. It computes, from real computed styles:
- WCAG contrast ratios (actual relative-luminance math against the *effective* background — see below — not a pixel-color guess)
- Presence/absence of accessible names on interactive elements
- Touch-target sizing against the WCAG 2.5.8 minimum
- Color-only link indicators
- Table row density and active-nav-state counts

Selectors are generic (`button, a, input, [role="button"]`, `[aria-current]`) rather than app-specific, so the script doesn't quietly break the next time unrelated markup changes. Findings that share a root cause are grouped into one row with a count instead of one row per element — a 300-row table shouldn't produce 300 near-identical findings.

**The skill itself.** Orchestrates the run: confirms scope with the user, executes the capture test, verifies the screenshot is actually fresh (not a stale leftover from a previous run) before trusting it, reviews it across six lenses, and always produces both a structured HTML report and an inline chat summary table.

## The six lenses

Visual hierarchy & clutter, navigation & wayfinding, consistency, information density, actionability of primary actions, and accessibility. The last is fully measurement-backed; hierarchy/clutter and consistency remain genuine judgment calls, but are supported by objective counts (element counts, distinct font families, distinct border-radius values) rather than judgment in a vacuum.

## The engineering story worth telling

The first working version already claimed to cover "accessibility" as one of its six lenses. It didn't hold up: pressed on whether it had actually accounted for accessibility, the honest answer was that it had only made a visual judgment call ("contrast looks fine"). A quick manual pixel-sampling pass against the same screenshot found three real WCAG AA contrast failures the vision-only pass had missed entirely — a green primary action button, a numeric status badge, and an active navigation item, all under the 4.5:1 threshold.

That prompted rebuilding the accessibility lens on top of real computed-style extraction instead of a screenshot judgment call. That rebuild surfaced two more bugs worth noting:

1. **Background resolution.** Contrast math needs the *effective* background behind a piece of text, not the element's own background. Plenty of interactive elements have a transparent background of their own and visually sit on a colored ancestor panel several DOM levels up (a sidebar link, for instance, sitting on a dark panel rather than the page's white background). The first version fell back straight to the page background when an element's own background was transparent, which misjudged plenty of perfectly legible text as failing. Fixed by walking up the DOM to the nearest ancestor with an actual opaque background.
2. **Finding volume.** Once real measurement replaced eyeballing, a data-dense page produced over a thousand lines of near-duplicate findings (every small numeric link in a large table individually flagged as an undersized touch target). Fixed with signature-based grouping so systemic issues surface as one finding with a count, not a wall of repetition.

The throughline: a lens being *listed* in a report's spec is not the same as it being *rigorously checked* — worth stating explicitly rather than letting a plausible-sounding report imply more rigor than it has.

## Current state

Capture test, signal-extraction module, and the orchestrating skill are built and working end to end against a live environment. Not yet built: broader page coverage beyond a single view (see below), and a live re-verification pass specifically targeting the background-resolution and grouping fixes on a second real page.

