## Secondary: pairing this with lightweight cross-page sweeps

The functional end-to-end suite this reuses infrastructure from is deep, not wide — it drives a handful of core transactional flows through real business logic, and in doing so visits only the small number of pages those flows touch. Large parts of a typical application (reports, messaging, support, administration, settings) are never visited by automation at all, simply because there's no business-logic reason for a lifecycle test to go there.

The idea under discussion: a separate, deliberately lightweight file of Playwright tests whose entire job is to *move from link to link* — no forms, no data setup, no business assertions — purely to land the browser on a page long enough to run the same signal-extraction module already built for this skill. Where a full lifecycle test might take minutes to set up the state needed to reach a page, a link-hop test costs a login and a couple of clicks, so far more of the application can be swept per run.

**Why this is worth doing, not just cheap:** run regularly (alongside the existing automation, e.g. on the same schedule or PR-preview trigger), each sweep's signals can be stored and diffed against a baseline over time. That turns this from a one-off audit into a continuous design-regression detector — catching things like "a component's color changed and a button now fails contrast" or "a new page shipped with no accessible names on its primary actions" automatically, the same way functional tests catch behavioral regressions, rather than waiting for the next manual review.

**The constraint that actually shapes the design:** the existing suite runs in parallel (pytest-xdist), so any new tests have to assume no shared state and no guaranteed ordering between separate test functions. Two patterns were considered:

- **Independent hops (preferred for broad coverage).** Each test function logs in and navigates directly to its one target page. Slightly more repetition (a login and a couple of clicks per test), but fully isolated and safe under parallel execution — this is the default choice for sweeping as many pages as possible.
- **Single connected journey.** One test function captures signals at multiple points along a single navigation path (e.g., landing page → a list view → a detail view). Still one self-contained test, so it doesn't introduce cross-test ordering dependencies, but it's reserved for cases where the *transition itself* is what's being evaluated — does the user lose their place, does a breadcrumb update correctly, does "back" behave sensibly — rather than being used as a shortcut to visit more pages per test.

**What this doesn't replace:** a scripted sweep, however broad, is still not a substitute for real user testing or a full manual accessibility audit — no screen-reader behavior, no keyboard-only navigation, no real assistive technology in the loop. The value is a fast, repeatable, low-cost first-pass safety net that can run continuously, not a replacement for deeper review.
